  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Dry and increasingly warm weather expected through \nFriday, followed by high rain chances over the weekend, including \nthe potential for localized heavy rainfall.\n\nSurface high pressure centered over the Mid Atlantic region early \nthis afternoon will gradually shift eastward toward Bermuda through \nthe rest of the work week, while continuing to extend across central \nNC. A mid level perturbation swinging through the base of the \nweakening trough axis stretching from New Brunswick through central \nNC to the FL panhandle is supporting sct-bkn cloudiness this \nafternoon, particularly over our N areas, but these clouds should \nbreak up and dissipate into this evening as the wave departs. Latest \nsurface dewpoints are 2-7 deg F higher than at this time yesterday, \nand this increasing low level moisture (especially across our S and \nE), the dearth of clouds, and calm air overnight will foster \nformation of areas of ground fog late tonight, with the highest \nconfidence across the N and E. Lows will be about a category above \nnormal, from the mid 50s to lower 60s. \n\nDeeply dry air and low PW will stay in place through the work week. \nThe mid level trough axis will weaken further and shift to our E and \nSE, as the strengthening ridge aloft builds from the lower Miss \nValley across the Mid South and Carolinas, before anchoring just off \nthe Southeast coast by Thu night/Fri. Lower level (925-850 mb) \nridging extending E-W across the Carolinas during this period will \nkeep us largely cut off from a significant tap of Gulf and Atlantic \nmoisture, with PW values expected to remain below normal and the \nbetter moisture shunted to our W and N, keeping our weather \ngenerally dry through Fri. Low level thicknesses will trend up to \n~10 m above normal Wed and ~20 m above normal Thu/Fri, favoring \nhighs in the 80s to lower 90s, hottest on Fri.\n\nA pattern change to wet weather is expected by the weekend. The deep \nclosed low now near Las Vegas will shift E through the Southwest and \nS Plains while weakening as it lifts NE into the Great Lakes region, \nleaving a baggy trough trailing back to W TX. This dampening wave \nwill lose steam as it encounters the Southeast ridge, thus the \nbetter rain chances late Fri night through Sat morning will still be \nto our W and N. But then polar stream energy digging into the Upper \nMidwest and W Great Lakes will dig broad troughing over the eastern \nGreat Lakes and Northeast by late Sat through Sun night, nudging the \nwesterlies southward into the Mid Atlantic region, tamping down the \nSoutheast ridge, pushing a surface cold front into NC, and allowing \nfor an increasing and deepening moisture flux from FL and the Gulf. \nModels still differ a bit on the details but agree on a surge of \nhigh PW (180%-200% of normal) by Sat afternoon, peaking late Sat \nnight through Sun evening, and forecast soundings show an LCL-0C \ndepth over 4 km, favoring warm rain processes and boosting the \npotential for heavy rainfall, particularly with any training rain \nclusters, with the highest pops on Sun. This possible threat will be \nclosely monitored in the coming days. Sunday should be markedly \ncooler as the surface cold front settles into our S and E, with rain \nfalling over our area and a surface low tracking along the front. \nCool Canadian high pressure will then build in from the NW and N by \nTue, getting us back into dry weather.\n\nAfter over 36 years in the NWS, this will be my final forecast \ndiscussion. It's been my pleasure to serve the people of central NC \nfor so many years. I wish you all calm and clear weather! -Hartfield",
